<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ziggurat">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ziggurat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A ziggurat is a massive stepped temple tower built in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, with a shrine on its summit. Ziggurats were constructed of mud brick, often faced with baked brick, and served as the centre of a city's religious life. The great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur">ziggurat of Ur</a> was built under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>, and the Elamite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/untash-napirisha">Untash-Napirisha</a> built the ziggurat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chogha-zanbil">Chogha Zanbil</a>.</p></div>
